# Pixi managed env (pixi.lock IS committed for reproducibility)
.pixi/

# Working build directory — `make` writes the fresh render to
# build/<slug>.pdf and (with `make html`) build/<slug>.html. The committed
# reference PDF lives at the repo ROOT as <slug>.pdf (downloadable from
# GitHub); nothing under build/ is tracked.
build/

# Opt-in web layer (present only after `bootstrap.py --with-web`). The site is
# rendered to app/dist/ by `make web`; nothing under it is tracked. Node deps
# for the wrangler dev/deploy wrapper live in app/node_modules — the lockfile
# IS committed for reproducibility, but node_modules is not.
app/dist/
node_modules/

# Secrets, and the files the local Cloudflare workflow writes. README's
# "Security notes" tells the reader never to commit a token to `.env` — and
# nothing here enforced it, so the warning was the only thing standing between a
# `git add -A` and a published credential. `.dev.vars` and `.wrangler/` are not
# hypothetical either: `make dev` runs `wrangler dev`, which reads the first for
# local bindings and writes its own state into the second.
.env
.env.local
.env.*.local
.dev.vars
.dev.vars.*
.wrangler/

# Python
__pycache__/
*.pyc
*.pyo
.venv/
venv/
.pytest_cache/
.coverage
htmlcov/

# Node — the jsdom nav test runs under `npm ci`, and both of these are its
# by-products rather than anything the kit authors.
npm-debug.log*
coverage/

# OS clutter
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# Editor
.vscode/
.idea/
*.swp
*~
# Local Claude Code state (settings.local.json, session scratch) — but NOT the kit-owned
# rules and skills, which are source: sync.py copies them into every guide, so a clone
# without them would silently sync nothing.
#
# `.claude/*` rather than `.claude/`: git cannot re-include a file whose parent DIRECTORY is
# excluded, so the negations below only work against a pattern that excludes the children.
.claude/*
!.claude/rules/
!.claude/skills/

# A personal transcript-scraping script that lives beside this checkout and has
# nothing to do with building guides. Ignored rather than deleted: it is
# somebody's working tool, and being the repository's only untracked file made it
# one `git add -A` away from being published as if it were part of the kit.
tools/session-history.sh

# NOTE: `*-slides.pdf` was ignored here while the deck deliberately had no
# committed reference. It does now — `baseline.py --artifact` plus baseline.yml's
# per-artifact loop are what made that safe — so the reference must NOT be
# ignored, or the one file the whole refresh path exists to maintain would be
# silently unstageable.
